.TH CHMAP_INSERT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_insert \- insert a key-value pair (type-inferred)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_insert(hm_name, key, val);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-inferred wrapper for chmap_insert_elem() that builds the cmap_pair
for the key and the cmap_pair for the value from the given expressions.
When the map already holds the key, the macro updates the value silently;
it calls ccol_fatal_err() only on a true failure, which is
ccol_not_enough_memory, ccol_container_full or ccol_invalid_args.

key and val go through the DECLARED key type and value type of the
map: the macro makes a private copy of each argument with that type by
a plain C assignment, so the value is CONVERTED and is never
reinterpreted byte for byte. A float that goes into a map of int
becomes 1 for 1.5f, just as in "int x = 1.5f;", and does not become
1069547520. An argument whose type has no implicit conversion to the
declared type is a compile error at the call site. cvec_push(3)
follows the same rule.

Because the macro copies the argument rather than taking its address,
an lvalue, an rvalue, a literal and a computed expression are all
accepted.

A declared key or value type that is a CHARACTER POINTER is the one
exception, and it converts nothing. char *, signed char * and
unsigned char * are one single type to this library: all three
classify as ccol_string, a NUL-terminated string, which the macro
measures with strlen. The map compares the bytes of the string and
never the pointer type, so a map keyed on unsigned char * stays
reachable through a char * that addresses the same string. An array
key, such as a char buf[64] or a string literal, decays to a pointer
just as it does in any other assignment.

A macro cannot know the length of a buffer that a pointer addresses,
so a character pointer is never a binary key. For a binary key, use one
of two forms. A key of a fixed length, such as a digest, can be a
struct that wraps an array, such as
struct { unsigned char b[32]; }; the map hashes and compares all of its
bytes, zero bytes included. A key whose length is known only at run
time goes through the raw chmap_insert_elem(3) layer, with that
length in cmap_pair.size.

A NULL character pointer is not a string, so a NULL key or value of a
character-pointer type stops the program with ccol_invalid_args.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Hash map to insert into
key      Key to insert
val      Value to associate with key
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program only on a true failure; an update of an existing key
is not a failure
Copies the key and the value, so neither has to be addressable
Handles both value types and string types correctly
For a float or double key, equality is bitwise, with one exception:
the map canonicalizes -0.0 and 0.0 to the same key. A NaN key obeys
bitwise equality rather than IEEE 754 equality (see
chmap_construct(3))
For a long double key, equality uses the value (native ==) rather than
the bytes, which every other key type uses. Every NaN long double
collapses into one key (see chmap_construct(3))
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(map, int, char*);
int first = 42;
chmap_insert(map, first, "hello");
chmap_insert(map, 100, "world");   /* a literal key is fine */
chmap_destroy(map);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_insert_elem (3),
.BR chmap_get (3),
.BR chmap_remove (3)
